<article class="section"><div class="container" style="max-width:800px;margin:0 auto;">
<div class="reveal">
<p>Xinjiang is the most climatically extreme province of China. You might leave Kashgar at 20°C in the morning and reach Karakul Lake at 3,600m with snow flurries by noon. Turpan is a furnace in summer; the high-speed train carriage is arctic air-conditioned. Packing wrong here isn't uncomfortable — it can spoil the trip. Here's exactly what we tell our own passengers to bring.</p>

<h2>The #1 Rule: Layers, Layers, Layers</h2>
<p>Across a single Silk Road day in Xinjiang you can experience 25°C of temperature swing. Mornings at the Kanas lake are near-freezing; afternoons in Turpan hit 40°C. The winning system: <strong>base layer + fleece + wind shell</strong>, all easy to peel off and tie around the waist. Skip the single heavy parka — it's useless when you overheat.</p>

<h2>Documents & Money (Don't Forget These)</h2>
<ul style="color:var(--text-light);line-height:1.8;padding-left:20px;">
<li><strong>Passport (original, 6-month validity):</strong> Non-negotiable. You'll be asked for it at every hotel and train station.</li>
<li><strong>Chinese visa:</strong> Check if your passport qualifies for visa-free entry (China has a 30-day visa-free policy for 40+ countries including the US, EU, UK, Australia). If not, apply before departure.</li>
<li><strong>Border permit:</strong> Required for Karakul Lake, the Karakoram Highway to Pakistan, and any Tajik/Kyrgyz border area. Your hotel or tour operator arranges this in Kashgar in 1–2 working days — bring your passport.</li>
<li><strong>Some cash (CNY):</strong> Cards and Alipay barely work outside Urumqi's big hotels. Bring ¥1,500–3,000 in small bills for bazaars, drivers, and national park entry. ATMs exist in every county town.</li>
<li><strong>Printed hotel/tour confirmations:</strong> Chinese registration form occasionally needs a paper copy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Clothing by Season</h2>
<p><strong>Spring (Apr–May) / Autumn (Sep–Oct):</strong> T-shirts, a warm fleece, a wind- and water-resistant shell, one pair of long trousers, one pair of shorts, a swimsuit (for the hotel spa), comfortable walking shoes. Nights in Kashgar are 5–10°C — pack a beanie and gloves for the Pamir day trip.</p>
<p><strong>Summer (Jun–Aug):</strong> Turpan and the Flaming Mountains can hit 45°C. Bring breathable lightweight shirts, a wide-brim hat, and sunscreen — but still pack the fleece for the mountains and the air-conditioned train.</p>
<p><strong>Winter (Dec–Feb):</strong> Kashgar and Urumqi are -5 to -15°C. Insulated parka, thermal base layers, down vest, warm gloves, wool socks, and sturdy winter boots. Kanas in winter is a frozen wonderland but brutally cold.</p>

<InlineCta />

<h2>Sun & Dust Protection (Essential, Not Optional)</h2>
<p>Xinjiang's desert UV is brutal. At 3,600m on the Pamir Highway the atmosphere filters almost nothing.</p>
<ul style="color:var(--text-light);line-height:1.8;padding-left:20px;">
<li><strong>SPF 50+ sunscreen</strong> — reapply every 2 hours, including on your ears and neck.</li>
<li><strong>Polarized sunglasses</strong> — essential for snow/desert glare and driving.</li>
<li><strong>Wide-brim hat or sun hood</strong> — a baseball cap isn't enough.</li>
<li><strong>Scarf, buff or shemagh</strong> — the single most useful item. Shields from desert dust, sun, and cold; doubles as a picnic blanket.</li>
<li><strong>Lip balm with SPF</strong> — the dry climate cracks lips in an hour.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Altitude & Health</h2>
<p>Most Xinjiang cities (Kashgar 1,290m, Urumqi 800m, Turpan -154m) are fine. But the Pamir day trip reaches 3,600m and the Karakoram Highway climbs to 4,693m at the Khunjerab Pass. Bring: any personal prescription medicines (copies of prescriptions), Diamox if you're sensitive to altitude, rehydration salts, and a basic first-aid kit (band-aids, antidiarrheal, painkillers). Drink water constantly — the air humidity is often under 20%.</p>

<h2>Electronics & Connectivity</h2>
<ul style="color:var(--text-light);line-height:1.8;padding-left:20px;">
<li><strong>Universal travel adapter (Type A/I):</strong> China uses 220V. Bring a small power strip — hotel rooms rarely have enough sockets.</li>
<li><strong>VPN:</strong> Google, WhatsApp, Instagram, and Facebook are blocked in mainland China. Install and test your VPN <em>before</em> arrival — app stores block VPN downloads in China.</li>
<li><strong>Offline maps:</strong> Download maps.me or the offline region in Google Maps. Cell signal vanishes in the mountains.</li>
<li><strong>Translation app:</strong> Download offline Chinese language packs in Google Translate or Pleco.</li>
<li><strong>Power bank (20,000mAh):</strong> Long drives on the Karakoram Highway last 6–8 hours with no charging.</li>
<li><strong>Camera + extra battery:</strong> Cold drains batteries fast; keep spares in an inner pocket.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What NOT to Bring</h2>
<p>Skip heavy guidebooks (use your phone), expensive jewelry (leave valuables in the hotel safe), more than one pair of shoes, and any drone — drones are heavily restricted in Xinjiang without a special permit, and flying one without permission leads to serious trouble. Don't bring pork products or alcohol into Uyghur areas out of respect for local customs.</p>

<h2>A Final Tip From Our Drivers</h2>
<p>Our local drivers have made the Kashgar–Pamir run over a thousand times. Their advice: put the scarf, sunscreen, power bank, and a bottle of water in a small day bag that stays in the car, not your suitcase. You'll thank us at 4,000m on the way to Karakul Lake.</p>

<p style="margin-top:32px;">Still not sure what to pack for your dates? Send us your itinerary — we'll send back a tailored packing list before you fly.</p>
</div></article>

